WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.180
How does naive string matching

2
00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:03.960
scan the text? We will

3
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.140
align a pattern, compare characters,

4
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.080
reset after mismatches, and derive

5
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.340
the scan bound from the

6
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.820
number of legal alignments.

7
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.140
Our output is the first

8
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.940
starting index where every pattern

9
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.820
character matches a consecutive text

10
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.260
window. We will use zero-based

11
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.640
indices here, with text A

12
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.600
B A B A B

13
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.520
A C and pattern A

14
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:27.680
B A C.

15
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.080
A legal starting index i

16
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.880
ranges from zero through n

17
00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.240
minus m inclusive. If the

18
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.360
pattern is longer than the

19
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.020
text, there is no complete

20
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.060
window, so return no match

21
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.920
without reading past the text.

22
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.840
At start zero, compare A

23
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.120
with A, B with B,

24
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.080
and A with A successfully.

25
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.980
The fourth comparison checks pattern

26
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.900
C against text B and

27
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.600
fails, so that window is

28
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.160
not a match.

29
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.980
Move the entire pattern one

30
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.220
position right and restart at

31
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.340
its first character. At start

32
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.140
one, pattern A immediately disagrees

33
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.140
with text B, so reject

34
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.540
after one comparison rather than

35
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.520
continuing from the previous mismatch.

36
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.000
At start two, the first

37
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.220
three characters again match A,

38
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.160
B and A. Pattern C

39
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.280
then disagrees with text B,

40
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.160
using four comparisons for this

41
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:21.580
alignment.

42
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.640
At start three, A immediately

43
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.900
disagrees with B, so reject

44
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.020
with one comparison. At start

45
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.060
four, all four characters A,

46
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.880
B, A, C match, so

47
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.380
return index four as the

48
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.080
first occurrence.

49
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.400
The trace used four plus

50
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.100
one plus four plus one

51
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.580
plus four comparisons, for fourteen

52
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.080
in total. Count actual character

53
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.400
checks separately from loop-control tests

54
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.880
when that is the question's

55
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:48.840
chosen operation.

56
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.600
For the worst-case pattern, place

57
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.300
many A characters before a

58
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.880
final C and search an

59
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.680
all-A text. Each legal alignment

60
00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.420
matches almost the whole pattern

61
00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.820
before failing on its last

62
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:01.320
character.

63
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.320
There are n minus m

64
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.780
plus one complete windows, and

65
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.660
each can require m character

66
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.880
comparisons. That gives a scan

67
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.360
upper bound of m times

68
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.020
n minus m plus one,

69
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.420
with the near-match family attaining

70
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.100
that order.

71
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.340
The straightforward implementation uses a

72
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.220
constant number of indices beyond

73
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.140
the input strings. A full

74
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.960
specification should also state an

75
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.700
empty-pattern convention; returning zero is

76
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.780
common, but blindly reading pattern

77
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.260
position zero when the pattern

78
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.360
is empty is invalid.

79
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.420
Things to take note of:

80
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.420
keep the alignment start distinct

81
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.860
from the current character pointers

82
00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.700
and reset the pattern after

83
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.540
a mismatch. Stop at the

84
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.500
last complete window, return the

85
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.220
first successful start, and do

86
00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.840
not confuse a subsequence with

87
00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.360
the consecutive substring we are

88
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:49.980
searching for, hmph.
